    body: `<blockquote>"Likewise the Spirit helps us in our weakness. For we do not know what to pray for as we ought, but the Spirit himself intercedes for us with groanings too deep for words." (Romans 8:26)</blockquote>
<p>There is a prayer you started and never finished. You know the one. You began it in the car, or standing at the sink, or in the dark with the ceiling above you, and somewhere in the middle the words ran out. Not because you gave up. Because you reached the edge of what you could say and there was nothing on the other side but the thing itself, too big to fit in a sentence.</p>
<p>I used to think that meant I was doing it wrong. That real prayer was fluent, that saints closed every petition with a clean amen. So I would push through, manufacture a few more lines, tie the whole thing off so it looked complete. I was performing prayer for an audience that already knew I was faking it. God was not fooled. He was waiting for me to stop.</p>
<p>Paul says the Spirit intercedes with groanings too deep for words. Read that slowly. The deepest prayers in the universe are not eloquent. They are groans. Before there was a theology of unanswered prayer, before anyone wrote a book on the discipline of it, there was a groan under the surface of the world, and the Spirit was carrying it up to the Father in a language older than speech.</p>
<p>This means the prayer you could not finish was not incomplete. It was handed off. The moment your words failed, another voice picked up the sentence and carried it the rest of the way. You were not abandoned at the edge of your vocabulary. You were met there.</p>
<p>I do not say this to make the silence comfortable. It is not comfortable to run out of words in front of God. It feels like failure and it feels like exposure. But it is the place where prayer stops being a thing you produce and becomes a thing you receive. The unfinished prayer is the one that finally admitted it needed help to be prayed at all.</p>
<p>So tonight, when the sentence trails off and you sit there feeling like you have nothing left, do not scramble for the ending. Let it hang there. The Spirit is not embarrassed by your silence. He is fluent in it. What you could not say, he is already saying, and he is saying it better than you would have, and he is saying it right now.</p>
<p>The prayer you cannot finish is not yours to finish. It never was.</p>`,
