    pillar: "Integrated Life",
    readTime: "4 min",
    body: `<blockquote>"Behold, I stand at the door and knock. If anyone hears my voice and opens the door, I will come in to him and eat with him, and he with me." (Revelation 3:20)</blockquote>
<p>You have built a picture of God in the back of your mind, and the picture is impatient. In it, he is at the door with his arms crossed, waiting for you to get your act together, checking the time. You have not prayed in a while. You have not opened the Bible. You have been living like he was not there, and now the thought of turning back carries a low dread, the dread of a man returning to a room where he expects to be scolded.</p>
<p>I know that picture because I lived inside it for years after I came to faith. I had left atheism behind, but I dragged the suspicion with me. I assumed that whatever God existed was mostly disappointed in me. So I stayed away from the table, not because I disbelieved, but because I could not imagine being welcome at it.</p>
<p>Read the verse again. He is not banging on the door demanding entry. He is knocking, and he says that if you open it, he will come in and eat with you. Eat with you. The image is not a courtroom. It is a shared meal, the slowest and most intimate thing two people do, the thing you only do with people you are not in a hurry to leave.</p>
<p>Jesus said this to a church that had gone cold, a church he had just rebuked for being lukewarm. And after the rebuke, this. Not, get out. But, I am at the door, and I would like to come in and eat with you. The rebuke and the invitation are the same voice. He names the coldness precisely because he wants back in.</p>
<p>The father in the parable did not tap his foot either. He ran. He saw the son while he was still a long way off and he ran to him, and there was a robe and a ring and a feast, and the only person angry about the meal was the brother who never left. God is the one preparing the table. We are the ones who assume the table is a trap.</p>
<p>So if you have been standing outside your own faith, rehearsing the scolding you expect, let the picture go. It was never his. The knock you keep hearing is not accusation. It is invitation. There is a chair pulled out, and it has been pulled out this whole time, and the food is not getting cold because he is willing to wait as long as it takes.</p>
<p>He is not at the door with crossed arms. He is at the table. Open it.</p>`,
